  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low-end chance for isolated showers and \nthunderstorms today, though no severe weather is expected.\n\nEarly morning radar shows a broken band of light but persistent \nshowers stretching from Brunswick County to Amherst County as a weak \nshortwave moves along the northwesterly flow aloft. The lightning \nhas diminished from the showers and they are light to locally \nmoderate in rainfall intensity at this hour. Some reporting sites in \nPrince Edward, Cumberland and Lunenburg Counties have recorded 0.05-\n0.25\" over the last few hours. Hi-res guidance keeps this off and on \nshowery activity across the Piedmont throughout the day. Can't rule \nout a few rumbles of thunder, especially into the afternoon hours, \nbut there is no severe weather expected today. Also of note, some \npatchy fog is developing this morning as well. High temperatures \nwill be a little cooler where the showers stick around; currently \nforecasting around 80 degrees across the western Piedmont and low to \n mid 80s to the east. Dewpoints will start to drop throughout the \nday today, making for a much nicer day than what we've been dealing \nwith lately. Shower chances will diminish this evening as high \npressure builds in from the north. Low temperatures Monday morning \nwill make you want to open the windows as lower 60s are in the \nforecast. Cooler spots in the NW could even dip into the upper\n50s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Much more seasonable temperatures to start the week, \nthen warming up for mid-late week. \n\nHigh pressure will continue to build down into our region on Monday \nallowing the pleasant weather to continue. Dewpoints will be in the \nlow to mid 60s with partly cloudy skies. High temperatures will once \nagain be in the lower 80s across the area. Expecting mostly dry \nconditions for Labor Day, however, hi-res guidance does hold onto a \nvery slight chance of a shower across eastern areas throughout the \nday. Wouldn't allow that to affect any holiday plans though since \nthe chance is very low. East-northeast winds may become a bit breezy \ntowards the coast, especially Hampton Roads and south into the \nnorthern NC beaches. If you're ring in Labor Day at the beach, \nplease be mindful that the rip current risk will likely be\nsignificant as onshore winds increase and nearshore waves\nbuild.Temperatures Monday night will drop into the upper 50s \ninland with mid 60s closer to the coast, making for a below \nnormal night across the area. \n\nTuesday looks very similar with dry conditions and highs in the mid \n80s persisting. Light winds will gradually become southeasterly \nthrough the day, helping keep skies mostly sunny. By Wednesday, our \narea of high pressure will start to slide offshore as a potent low \npressure system crosses into eastern Canada and its associated cold \nfront drops into the Mississippi River Valley . Here locally, the \nrepositioning of the ridge over the southern Mid-Atlantic and \nSoutheast will allow temperatures to warm up a few degrees as highs \ntop out in the upper 80s to around 90 degrees. Dewpoints will start \nto creep back as well, but will remain well below the oppressive \nlevels we saw at the end of last week. Dry conditions continue into \nthe start of Thursday, but rain chances currently look to make their \nreturn late Thursday into Friday as a cold front moves through. As \nof now, southwesterly flow will result in Thursday being our hottest \nday of the week with highs reaching the low to mid 90s (warmest \nacross the S/SW). Hopefully the cold front can knock highs back into \nthe 80s to end the week.",
